{
  "_comment": "Hand-transcribed from the Federal Reserve's own published FOMC calendars (current + historical archive pages). FRED has NO reliable API for this -- its 'FOMC Press Release' release (id 101) returns a release date for every single calendar day once no-data dates are included (verified live 2026-08-27), which is not a real schedule and must never be used as one. This file is therefore the manual half of daytrade/macro_calendar.py's three-source merge. `date` is the day the statement/decision was actually released -- the second day of a two-day meeting for regular meetings, or the release date itself for the 2020 unscheduled emergency actions. `press_conference` is true iff a Chair press conference followed same-day (verified against each year's fomcpresconf<date>.htm page existing on federalreserve.gov: quarterly only in 2016-2018 (Mar/Jun/Sep/Dec, aligned with the SEP), then every meeting from 2019 onward). The Mar 17-18, 2020 regular meeting was folded into the Mar 15, 2020 unscheduled emergency decision and formally cancelled -- no separate statement exists for it, so it is not listed here (verified against federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomchistorical2020.htm, which labels it '(cancelled)'). Coverage starts 2016 to match this repo's SPY/QQQ bar history depth (data/daytrade/bars_premarket, 2016-01-01 forward) -- years before 2016 were not transcribed because they fall outside any bar coverage this repo holds, not because they could not be sourced. Re-verify against source_url periodically; the Fed typically publishes a year of meetings at once and they rarely move. Two entries carry unscheduled: true (2020-03-03, 2020-03-15) -- the COVID emergency actions, each 12 days apart, which is real and sourced, not the FRED release-101 daily-fabrication signature. Each unscheduled entry carries its own source_url pointing at the specific Fed page for that meeting, on top of the file-level source_url/source_urls_historical. See daytrade/test_macro_calendar.py::test_real_fomc_calendar_has_no_fabricated_looking_pattern for the enforced discipline: unflagged dates must be >=20 days apart, any narrower gap requires unscheduled: true + its own source_url, and no gap may ever be <2 days under any flag -- that 2-day floor is the actual release-101 signature (a date for every calendar day) and nothing legitimate, scheduled or not, looks like it.",
